\f1\fs22 Word of Sarah\'92s blossoming gestation drifted from the mansion staff to the eager ears of Elvi tour operators and on to the wider world. Soon, even the concession stand purveyors\'97hawking ponchos, leather jackets, deep-fried everything, and hair treatments heavy in vitamin E, jojoba oil, and boot polish\'97were murmuring as they slathered product and grease on the faithful. The story spread with the persistence of a gas leak, infusing the entire Elvi collective with a feverish, rumour-driven zeal.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The identity of the father spread faster still. Weeks before Kathy provided any answer to Sarah\'92s question about paternity, the Elvi were already convinced. The father was the \'93cool cat\'94 from the Vegas Show, the one tortured for allegedly kidnapping Sarah, who on prime-time 
\f2\i C.Y.I.
\f1\i0  was still protecting Sarah, denying everything.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To the Elvi, Chanta was a fucking hero. Yes, a fucking hero. In circumstances as sacred as these, \'93taxing hero\'94 was considered blasphemous, so the community dialled their swearing down a notch. Mother-fucking hero. Thank you very much.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah had grown accustomed to the sounds of the mansion\'97tourist murmurs, echoes of excited children, the drone of scripted guides, the shuffle of feet on polished floors\'97so accustomed, she awoke in alarm when the usual noise vanished. Stranger still, the new sounds: something like a parade ground. Marching, bellowed orders, the grind of something heavy on the gravel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Before panic could freeze her, Sarah launched herself from bed as if catapulted\'97a trebuchet slap of linens and all. She crashed to the window above the portico just as a battalion of Elvi in camouflage ponchos snapped to attention. The once-manicured lawns had become a drill square.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Halt!\'94 barked a parade master beneath the portico. The formation snapped to with a cartoonish \'93right turn\'94 and, in perfect comic timing, \'93Present arms!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A hundred Elvi chopped at the air, hands up: judo salute, 101.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked. Surely things could not get more surreal.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then the tanks rolled up the driveway.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A dim part of Sarah\'92s brain begged her to hide under the blankets, even while she threw back the bedroom door, strong-armed Nancy out of her way, descended the staircase and hurled out the door to confront the enemy, in a t-shirt and yesterday\'92s underwear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Back turned, the parade master barked, \'93Army manual, section 43: when in hand-to-hand combat, apply judo chop to the enemy\'92s neck.\'94 The battalion performed a halting mass judo chop and waited.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s vocabulary failed her. \'93What the tax\'97who the\'97?\'94 she blurted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Forgive me, ma\'92am. We hope we didn\'92t wake you,\'94 the parade master said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah flushed, tried to flatten her t-shirt and rake her wild hair into submission.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Allow me to introduce myself. Specialist Five Tulsa McLean, at your service.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s thighs went cold; she debated crossing her knees, but held her ground. \'93
\f2\i My
\f1\i0  service?\'94 Despite (or maybe because of) the absurdity, she felt faintly flattered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92re here for your protection, ma\'92am.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93With tanks?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tulsa gave a lopsided grin. \'93Had a couple of spares, so why not defend headquarters?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Headquarters\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For the first time, Tulsa looked down and noticed the gooseflesh on Sarah\'92s legs. \'93Forgive me, ma\'92am. You\'92re freezing.\'94 He shepherded her confused and compliant indoors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the mansion\'92s echoing lobby, Sarah regathered her wits. \'93You seriously have more tanks?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, ma\'92am.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jaw set, Sarah\'92s brain spun, processing at battle tempo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Best if you don\'92t think too much on all this,\'94 Tulsa said, eyes flicking to her belly. \'93We\'92re looking after you\'97and everyone you care about.\'94
